#Lead-In

Our starting definition is thus...

And where we depart from that definition is by considering a first case of language revitalization which involves much more than is captured by the minimal definition. The first case herein is a retelling of my own personal experiences. The Cheyenne language was the first language in my father's side of my family up until his generation; this is a story of how certain memories of mine were transformed after I learned some of the most simple components of Cheyenne.

#Story Time

My great-greatparents were Blanche and Henry Whiteshield, and they lived in Hammon, Oklahoma. When I was very young, my parents and I used to drive up from Texas and return to see family on both sides in Western Oklahoma. In the course of these trips, we would usually make visits to see my great-grandparents. My great-grandfather passed before his wife, and thereafter we would still come to see only her. Most of my memories of my great-grandmother are from these visits to her in their home, clouded in the haze of childhood. Not much of specificity survived the years in my memory into my twenties, except for visceral details, like the smell of the house itself, the sense of hours stretching on, and the sounds of conversation between Great-Grandma and my father.

A visit to Great-Grandma usually meant, after saying greetings and being entreated to some kid-conversation, my father then sitting and talking with her, in English, in the front of the house, while I wandered around inside exploring, as children will do. I was far enough away both in space and attention that I could not grasp the substance of their conversations, but the dimmed sounds of their voices still reached me in the other rooms. Frequently in between my father's sentences, Great Grandma would make a noise that was strange to me. “Nohoo,” she would say, the last syllable stretched long and falling quite low in pitch. 

I had no idea what this noise was, and why she made it so repeatedly. From its place in the flow of conversation, I could at least tell that it was a response to the things my dad was telling her. But the falling tone of it made it sound to my ears like a note of disapproval, concern, or regret. That was only my best guess. And since no one else questioned it, neither did I. I had the sense that Great Grandma's way of talking was only something to be accepted, not understood or emulated. But I would never forget the sound of her voice making that noise, over and over.

><+>< 

My grandmother, child of Henry and Blanche Whiteshield, was named Marcy. Unlike my great-grandmother, my Grandma Marcy lived near to us and was central to my raising. Cheyenne was her first language, but she also learned English at home, and at Chilocco Indian School. When she had her children, she did not raise them to speak Cheyenne. When her children had children, she did not teach Cheyenne to the grandchildren, except maybe a word here or there when asked. Anytime I asked her point blank to teach me Cheyenne, my Grandma Marcy would laugh, and ask me why I wanted to learn it. Before I could even begin to come up with any kind of answer, she would, in a somewhat joking manner, advise that I should spend time learning a language that would help me more in life, like Spanish or Chinese. Other times she would object that she couldn't teach me because it had been too long since she had spoken it, and it was a hard language to explain. But, I would sometimes overhear her having conversations in Cheyenne with her siblings on the phone. I wanted to be able to talk with her in that way. Towards the end of her life I had convinced her to teach me what words and phrases she could, but to speak with her in full conversation never came to pass before she died.

My grandmother had many mannerisms that I knew intimately. One that was especially peculiar was the way she'd make a noise, that came almost out of surprise, when she said something factually off or wrong. It would be spliced in quickly, like she was interrupting herself, almost alarmed, and involuntarily, like a hiccup. It would go, for example: "We have to go the store on Tuesday because they're closed on Monday -HEH!- ...it's closed on Tuesday. HEH!- ...no, Monday, it's Monday." Like my great-grandmother's strange noise, I didn't make too much of my grandmother's habit-- she was just different from other people, and I chalked it up to this kind of pure idiosyncrasy. It was one that came out much more regularly back when I was younger- after her parents and some of her siblings passed, and she stopped speaking Cheyenne as much even in the small pockets that it used to be reserved for in her life, this habit dropped off from her speech, to the point where I had almost forgotten, for a while, that I'd ever heard her do it.

><+>< 

In my late twenties, I started to casually look into Cheyenne language resources online. One day, I was scrolling through the Cheyenne Dictionary developed at Dull Knife College. I happened to see the following entry:

[Insert image of 'nóoo' entry]

I don't know how I managed to luck out in finding this particular entry, in a sea of so many. I was immediately transported back to those childhood visits to Hammon. One of the audio samples for the entry confirmed a sure family resemblance to that strange sound I had heard coming from Great-Grandma all those years ago. It only changed one thing, but it changed everything. What was once mystifying turned out to have such a simple solution. She was simply responding to the things my father was telling her with expressions of surprise. She was exclaiming, as if saying 'wow' or 'oh my' in English. This marked a radical, irreversible shift in the way I remember my great-grandmother. Her utterances had been decoded and she had become intelligible to me.

A few years later, scrolling through the same dictionary, I came upon another entry:

[Image of 'he' entry]

At the time, in an older version of the dictionary, I believe the entry just simply labeled this word a "self-corrective." Again, I still wonder to this day how lucky I am that my attention fell intently enough on this particular entry as well. Albeit a little more slowly in this case, time started to turn back in my head, and I remembered hearing my grandmother make this sound, in a way that I could then see, was completely congruous with an exclamation of self-correction or revision. Once again, a small mystery that I had assumed I'd never grasp, had been resolved, and the answer was astoundingly simple. Just this small fact changed how I saw my grandmother back in those days. She may not have embraced Cheyenne after her educational and other life experiences, but there were still certain things that stuck deep within her.

#Commentary

Learning just this one small piece of vocabulary meant the difference between recalling my great grandmother in a mysterious light and remembering her intelligibly. 

Of course, other scenarios share similar features, like overhearing people speaking in code and learning it afterwards, or even the mundane case of mishearing someone and only later realizing what they said. What is especially important in our story is that we see a relation of intelligibility created between an ancestor and her descendant. In the context of family, this relation of intelligibility makes it possible to do something very important: understand one’s kin.

Commentary on story-- an overcoming of alienation, in living memory, though both individuals in mind are no longer living. Also give some commentary on how exclamations and interjections are a really good place for starting out with revitalization and language learning in general. Interjections have a very high usage in everyday life and can therefore be very naturally integrated. 

(comment a little bit on why Nowak's view doesn't apply to this case, because the FL speakers are not living?)

